    "title": "Michael B. Jordan makes Oscar history as first Best Actor winner to play twins",
    "text": "Update: March 15 at 8:30 p.m. PTMichael B. Jordan made Oscars history on Sunday night as the first Best Actor winner to play twins.He took on the dual roles of twin brothers Smoke and Stack in Ryan Coogler's Sinners. The horror-musical also won Best Original Screenplay, Best Cinematography, and Best Score. See the Oscar winners list.More from Gold Derby(Yes, we know that, 60 years ago, Lee Marvin won the Best Actor Oscar for playing the dual roles of Kid Shelleen and Tim Strawn in Cat Ballou, but it was never confirmed on screen that those characters were twins. The implication was that they were brothers, but the script left out the specific \"twin\" label.)Original story: Jan. 22Michael B. Jordan notched his first Oscar nomination for pulling double duty on Sinners, becoming the latest Best Actor contender for playing multiple roles.In Ryan Coogler's critically acclaimed box-office hit, Jordan plays the Smokestack twins, Elijah and Elias. They are a pair of criminal brothers who return to their hometown of Clarksdale, Miss., in 1932. Soon after arriving and building their dream juke joint, they are confronted by blood-sucking, blues-loving vampires, resulting in an epic life-or-death struggle.Throughout Oscar history, eight men have previously received Best Actor bids for playing multiple roles. Two of them ended up winning:Fredric March in Dr. Jekyll & Mr. Hyde (1931/32) — winnerJosé Ferrer in Moulin Rouge (1952)Jack Lemmon in Some Like It Hot (1959)Peter Sellers in Dr. Strangelove (1964)Lee Marvin in Cat Ballou (1965) — winnerWarren Beatty in Heaven Can Wait (1978)Dustin Hoffman in Tootsie (1982)Nicolas Cage in Adaptation (2002)Critics agree that Jordan's performance(s) are nomination worthy. Kristy Puchko (Mashable) thinks Jordan \"gives not just the performance of his career, but two of them.\" Johnny Oleksinski (New York Post) feels the actor played the Smokestack twins \"both convincingly and charismatically.\" Justin Chang (NPR) says that Jordan \"subtly captures their crucial difference in temperament and worldview.\"Jordan is joined in the Best Actor field by Timothée Chalamet (Marty Supreme), Leonardo DiCaprio (One Battle After Another), Ethan Hawke (Blue Moon), and Wagner Moura (The Secret Agent).Sinners smashed the all-time record with 16 nominations this year, eclipsing the previous mark of 14 jointly held by All About Eve (1950), Titanic (1997), and La La Land (2016).Best of Gold DerbySign up for Gold Derby's Newsletter. For the latest news, follow us on Facebook, Twitter, and Instagram.Click here to read the full article.",
